  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1012 PM MDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nUpper high now over the southeast Gulf states. Ridging still nosing \nback toward southern New Mexico. Though moisture component is good \nover all the CWA, the best moisture still concentrates west of the \nRG Valley. Thus our early convection for this evening has been in \nthat area. Hi res models show that changing though late tonight as \nisolated storms move into the eastern half of the CWA. \n\nTuesday through Thursday...the upper high moves back westward, to \nTexArk region by Thursday. Initially this rids the high center of \nthe western ridge, so that deep moisture is better distributed \nacross all of the CWA. PWs will be in the 1.3 to 1.5 inch range.\nTuesday and Wednesday look to be the best days for area wide storms \nand the risk of flash flooding. A Flash Flood Watch is in effect for \nthe Gila/Black Range for Tuesday, and other areas will have to be \nlooked at for a potential watch (perhaps more so on Wednesday). \nModels have sped the high's westward movement up some, so by \nThursday the high is close enough (TexArk area) that moisture may \nbe nudged more out to the western CWA again. \n\nFriday through the weekend...the upper high migrates down to \nsouthern Texas into Mexico. Meanwhile over our area the \nmoisture/short waves gradually get entrained into a mid-latitude low \nand trough over the Great Basin. This will slowly shunt the moisture \nback east over the forecast area Friday for a brief uptick in storm \nactivity. This trough will move across New Mexico Saturday. Drying \nwill begin Saturday, though still enough moisture for \nisolate/scattered thunderstorms. Pattern recognition would suggest \npossible severe storms, but parameters don't look to much out of the \nordinary. By Sunday, another Pacific low entering California will \nprovide a dry punch into our area with very little chance of storms.\n\nBy Monday and Tuesday, both GFS/ECMWF show the California low moving \nover Nevada/Arizona, drawing up sub-tropical moisture and increasing \nthe chance of rain again.",
